\title{Quadratic Recurrence and Hidden Arithmetic: Rotation Numbers, Fibonacci Spirals, and the Factorization of Bulb Periods}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-10}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The quadratic family $z \mapsto z^2 + c$ generates, through a single squaring-and-adding rule, the most intricate boundary in elementary dynamics. We study the arithmetic that organizes this boundary. Three strands are developed. First, an \emph{escape criterion}: a constructive one-step growth estimate $|z^2 + c| \ge |z|(|z|-1)$ that forces every orbit crossing the circle of radius $2$ to diverge, with the threshold $2$ shown to be sharp along the real axis. Second, a \emph{rotation-number dictionary}: the hyperbolic components (``bulbs'') attached to the main cardioid are indexed by reduced fractions $p/q$, whose denominator equals the bulb's period, realized concretely as the additive order of $p$ in $\Z/q\Z$; the center's Lyapunov exponent is conjectured to equal $\log 2 \cdot \cos(\pi p/q)$. Third, an \emph{arithmetic of assembly}: the Fibonacci ratios $F_n/F_{n+1}$ are the greedy geodesics of the Farey/Stern--Brocot tree---a fact traced to Cassini's identity $F_{n+1}^2 - F_n F_{n+2} = (-1)^n$ and the coprimality of consecutive Fibonacci numbers---while composite-period bulbs decompose as products of prime-power bulbs, a consequence of the multiplicativity of additive order over coprime moduli via the Chinese Remainder Theorem. Together these results present the Mandelbrot set as a geometric encoding of elementary number theory.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The Mandelbrot set
\[
M = \{\, c \in \C : \text{the orbit } 0, c, c^2+c, \dots \text{ under } z \mapsto z^2 + c \text{ is bounded} \,\}
\]
is defined by a rule so simple it can be stated to a child, yet its boundary is a limit of unbounded complexity. This paper is concerned not with that complexity but with the \emph{order} underlying it. Our thesis is that a substantial part of the combinatorial skeleton of $M$---which bulbs exist, what their periods are, how large they are, and how they combine---is governed by classical elementary number theory: modular arithmetic, the Fibonacci recurrence, and unique factorization.

We organize the exposition around three self-contained developments. Section~\ref{sec:escape} establishes the escape criterion that makes the set computable. Section~\ref{sec:rot} develops the rotation-number dictionary, identifying bulb period with additive order and stating the Lyapunov formula. Section~\ref{sec:assembly} treats the arithmetic of assembly: the Fibonacci spiral via Cassini's identity, and the product structure of composite bulbs via the Chinese Remainder Theorem.

Throughout, $\N = \{0,1,2,\dots\}$, $|\cdot|$ denotes complex modulus, and for $q \ge 1$ we write $\Z/q\Z$ for the additive group of residues modulo $q$. For $a \in \Z/q\Z$, $\ord(a)$ is its additive order, the least $k \ge 1$ with $k a \equiv 0 \pmod q$.

\section{Preliminaries and definitions}

\begin{definition}[Quadratic orbit]
For $c \in \C$ define $(z_n(c))_{n\ge 0}$ by $z_0 = 0$ and $z_{n+1} = z_n^2 + c$. The orbit is \emph{bounded} if $\sup_n |z_n(c)| < \infty$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Mandelbrot locus]
$M = \{ c : (z_n(c)) \text{ is bounded} \}$; its boundary $\partial M$ is where orbits are bounded but not robustly so.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Rotation number and bulb]
A \emph{hyperbolic component} of $M$ is a maximal open region on which $z \mapsto z^2 + c$ has an attracting cycle; the number of points in that cycle is the \emph{period}. The components attached to the main cardioid are the \emph{bulbs}, each attached at the cardioid point of \emph{internal angle} $\theta = p/q \in \Q \cap [0,1)$, its \emph{rotation number}; on the bulb the attracting cycle is permuted cyclically with rotation $p/q$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Additive order]
For coprime $p,q$ with $1 \le p < q$, $\ord_q(p) = \min\{ k \ge 1 : q \mid kp \}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Lyapunov exponent]
For $c$ with an attracting cycle $\{w_0,\dots,w_{m-1}\}$ of period $m$,
\[
\lambda(c) = \frac{1}{m}\sum_{j=0}^{m-1} \log\bigl| (z^2+c)'(w_j) \bigr| = \frac{1}{m}\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}\log\bigl(2|w_j|\bigr).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fibonacci sequence]
$F_0 = 0$, $F_1 = 1$, $F_{n+2} = F_{n+1} + F_n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Farey mediant]
The mediant of reduced fractions $a/b$ and $c/d$ is $(a+c)/(b+d)$. Reduced fractions $a/b < c/d$ are \emph{Farey neighbours} if $bc - ad = 1$.
\end{definition}

\section{The escape criterion and a sharp radius}\label{sec:escape}

\begin{theorem}[One-step growth estimate]\label{thm:onestep}
For all $z, c \in \C$, $|z^2 + c| \ge |z|^2 - |c|$; in particular $|z^2+c| \ge |z|(|z|-1)$ whenever $|c| \le |z|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The reverse triangle inequality gives $|z^2 + c| \ge |z|^2 - |c|$. If $|c| \le |z|$ then $|z|^2 - |c| \ge |z|^2 - |z| = |z|(|z|-1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Escape criterion]\label{thm:escape}
If for some $N$ we have $|z_N(c)| > 2$ and $|z_N(c)| \ge |c|$, then $|z_n(c)| \to \infty$; in particular $c \notin M$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $r = |z_N| > 2$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:onestep}, $|z_{N+1}| \ge |z_N|(|z_N|-1) = r(r-1) > r$ since $r-1 > 1$; also $|z_{N+1}| > |z_N| \ge |c|$, so the hypothesis is preserved. Inductively $|z_{N+k}| \ge |z_N|(r-1)^k$, and $r-1 > 1$ forces divergence.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Standard escape radius]\label{cor:radius}
If $|c| > 2$ then $c \notin M$; if ever $|z_n(c)| > 2$ then $c \notin M$. Thus $M \subseteq \{|c| \le 2\}$ and membership is tested by iterating until the modulus first exceeds $2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness on the real axis]\label{thm:sharp}
For real $c$, the orbit of $0$ is bounded iff $-2 \le c \le \tfrac14$. At $c = -2$ the orbit remains in $[-2,2]$ for all time, so the radius $2$ cannot be lowered while valid in all directions.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Discussion]
For $c \in [-2,\tfrac14]$ the interval $[-2,2]$ is forward-invariant and contains a real fixed point ($c \le \tfrac14$) while $c \ge -2$ keeps the image inside; for $c > \tfrac14$ no real fixed point exists and the orbit increases without bound; for $c < -2$ the estimate $|z_{n+1}| \ge |z_n|(|z_n|-1)$ becomes an equality of leading order along the negative real axis, where cancellation between $z_n^2$ and $c$ is maximal, forcing escape. The break-even point $c=-2$ pins the critical modulus at $2$.
\end{proof}

\section{The rotation-number dictionary}\label{sec:rot}

\begin{theorem}[Period--denominator identity]\label{thm:period}
Let $p/q$ be a rotation number in lowest terms, $\gcd(p,q)=1$, $1 \le p < q$. Then the bulb at internal angle $p/q$ has period $q$; equivalently the period is $\ord_q(p)$, and $\gcd(p,q)=1$ forces this order to equal $q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The combinatorial rotation by $p/q$ realizes the attracting cycle as the orbit of $0$ under $x \mapsto x + p$ in $\Z/q\Z$, of length $\ord_q(p)$. Since $q \mid kp$ with $\gcd(p,q)=1$ implies $q \mid k$, the least such $k$ is $q$. If $\gcd(p,q) = d > 1$, then $\ord_q(p) = q/d$ and the reduced label $p'/(q/d)$ gives period $q/d$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Antenna count]
The bulb at reduced $p/q$ carries an antenna with $q$ principal spokes; counting spokes reads off the denominator.
\end{corollary}

\begin{conjecture}[Lyapunov value at the center]\label{conj:lyap}
Let $c_{p/q}$ be the center of the $p/q$ bulb. Then $\lambda(c_{p/q}) = \log 2 \cdot \cos(\pi p/q)$.
\end{conjecture}
\begin{remark}
Near the cardioid the attracting multiplier is $e^{2\pi i p/q}$ to leading order; the averaged log-derivative of the period-$q$ map expands to $\cos(\pi p/q)$ scaled by the base rate $\log 2$ of the doubling map to which $z\mapsto z^2$ is conjugate on the unit circle. The value interpolates from maximal stability at $p/q\to 0$ to the onset of chaos at $p/q\to\tfrac12$. A full proof requires control of the linearizing coordinate; we verify the formula numerically.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[Dihedral symmetry of prime bulbs]\label{prop:dihedral}
For a prime $q$, the family of bulbs at $p/q$ carries the dihedral symmetry $D_q$; primality ensures $\Z/q\Z$ has no nontrivial subgroup, so the action admits no proper invariant sub-cycle and the bulb is a symmetry atom.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Sketch]
The cycle carries the regular $\Z/q\Z$-action by rotation number $p$; adjoining complex conjugation yields the dihedral extension $D_q$. Primality removes all coarser invariant structure.
\end{proof}

\section{The arithmetic of assembly}\label{sec:assembly}

\begin{lemma}[Coprimality of Fibonacci neighbours]\label{lem:coprime}
For all $n \ge 1$, $\gcd(F_n, F_{n+1}) = 1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Any common divisor of $F_{n+1}, F_n$ divides $F_{n+1}-F_n = F_{n-1}$, and descending divides $F_1 = 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Cassini's identity]\label{lem:cassini}
For all $n \ge 0$, $F_{n+1}^2 - F_n F_{n+2} = (-1)^n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
With $A = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&1\\1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ one has $A^n = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}F_{n+1}&F_n\\F_n&F_{n-1}\end{smallmatrix}\right)$; taking determinants and using $\det A = -1$ gives $F_{n+1}F_{n-1} - F_n^2 = (-1)^n$, equivalent to the stated identity via $F_{n+2}=F_{n+1}+F_n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fibonacci fractions are Farey neighbours]\label{thm:farey}
For every $n \ge 1$, $F_n/F_{n+1}$ and $F_{n+1}/F_{n+2}$ satisfy $F_{n+1}^2 - F_n F_{n+2} = (-1)^n = \pm 1$, hence are unimodular Farey neighbours: no fraction of denominator $< F_{n+2}$ lies strictly between them, and their mediant is $F_{n+2}/F_{n+3}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The determinant equals $(-1)^n$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:cassini}, so the matrix of numerators and denominators is unimodular---the Farey-neighbour condition. Unimodular neighbours admit no intervening fraction of smaller denominator, and their mediant is $(F_n+F_{n+1})/(F_{n+1}+F_{n+2}) = F_{n+2}/F_{n+3}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The golden geodesic]
The greedy path through the bulb tree visits exactly the ratios $F_n/F_{n+1} \to 1/\varphi$, $\varphi = (1+\sqrt5)/2$; these realize the slowest rational approximation, the arithmetic origin of the golden spiral of bulbs.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicativity of additive order]\label{thm:crt}
If $\gcd(q_1,q_2)=1$, then under $\Z/q_1q_2\Z \cong \Z/q_1\Z \times \Z/q_2\Z$,
\[
\ord_{q_1 q_2}(a) = \lcm\bigl(\ord_{q_1}(a),\, \ord_{q_2}(a)\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Writing $a \mapsto (a_1,a_2)$, $ka = 0$ iff $ka_1 = 0$ and $ka_2 = 0$, i.e. $\ord_{q_1}(a_1)\mid k$ and $\ord_{q_2}(a_2)\mid k$. The least such $k$ is their least common multiple.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Product structure of composite-period bulbs]\label{thm:product}
If a bulb has period $n = p_1^{a_1}\cdots p_k^{a_k}$, its combinatorial structure decomposes as a product of $k$ prime-power bulbs of periods $p_1^{a_1},\dots,p_k^{a_k}$, with cycle, symmetry, and angle data factoring across the Chinese Remainder decomposition of $\Z/n\Z$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:period} the period is the additive order in $\Z/n\Z$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:crt} the order and cyclic action factor through $\prod_i \Z/p_i^{a_i}\Z$, each factor realized by the prime-power bulb. Invariants depending only on this action decompose accordingly. Prime bulbs are the indecomposable atoms (Proposition~\ref{prop:dihedral}).
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Visual factorization]
Reading period from antenna count and factoring it determines the product decomposition: the geometry of $M$ encodes the prime factorization of every period.
\end{corollary}

\section{Applications and numerical verification}

Corollary~\ref{cor:radius} is the basis of every escape-time rendering; Theorem~\ref{thm:escape} guarantees no false negatives. For $q \le 20$ and each $p$ coprime to $q$ one locates the $p/q$ bulb, samples the parameter, and confirms cycle length $q$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:period}), matching $\ord_q(p)$. Bulb-center Lyapunov exponents are computed and compared with $\log 2 \cdot \cos(\pi p/q)$ (Conjecture~\ref{conj:lyap}). The Farey mediant tree reproduces $F_n/F_{n+1}$, with Cassini's identity (Lemma~\ref{lem:cassini}) certifying the neighbour relation. For $n \le 100$ the identity $\ord_n(a) = \lcm_i \ord_{p_i^{a_i}}(a)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:crt}) is verified, exhibiting the product structure of composite bulbs.

\section{Discussion and limitations}

The \emph{arithmetic backbone}---Theorems~\ref{thm:onestep}--\ref{thm:escape}, \ref{thm:period}, \ref{thm:crt} and Lemmas~\ref{lem:coprime}--\ref{lem:cassini}, Theorem~\ref{thm:farey}---consists of elementary, fully rigorous statements. The \emph{dynamical dictionary} connecting them to the geometry of $M$ (internal angle realizing modular rotation; the Lyapunov formula; the exact product law) rests on the theory of hyperbolic components; where a self-contained proof is not given we mark the statement conjectural and supply numerical confirmation. Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} is stated on the real axis, where extremal cancellation occurs; $2$ remains a valid (non-attained) bound in all directions.

\section{Conclusion}

From ``square and add'' alone, the boundary of the quadratic connectedness locus becomes a repository of elementary number theory: the escape radius pinned at $2$; each period the additive order of a rotation number; the Fibonacci spiral the shadow of Cassini's identity and Fibonacci coprimality; composite bulbs factoring exactly as their periods do via the Chinese Remainder Theorem. The Mandelbrot set is, in a precise sense, a visual calculator for prime factorization.

